tml; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn41196view1\",\"pattern\":\"3 \"}); Total lines: 696\nL15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:     1. cite7†Contributions. L18:   3. cite8†2 Setting L19:     1. cite9†Prefill expert reduction. L20:     2. cite10†Models, tasks and pipelines. L21:     3. cite11†Prespecification. L22:   4. cite12†3 What Is Lost, and Where L23:     1. cite13†3.1 At half the experts, most of the F1 loss is form L24:     2. cite14†3.2 In Qwen3 the form is decided at the two tokens before the answer L25:     3. cite15†3.3 Other families: one end or the other L26:   5. cite16†4 Why These Tokens L136: ## 3 What Is Lost, and Where\nL137: \nL138: The interventions in this section use unmarked templates and their own baselines; Section cite22†5 turns to real marked prompts. Section cite13†3.1 uses 2,000 HotpotQA questions, on which the unmodified Qwen3 scores F1 69.81; the span experiments of Section cite14†3.2 and the family batch of Table cite99†1 are separate 2,000-question runs, in which the same uniform $8\\to 4$ costs $-$3.38 and $-$3.34, and single-token experiments use 500 questions.\nL139: ### 3.1 At half the experts, most of the F1 loss is form\nL144: ### 3.2 In Qwen3 the form is decided at the two tokens before the answer\nL150: Figure 2: Which tokens need their experts (Qwen3-30B-A3B, HotpotQA F1 against the unmodified model). (a) Keeping the last $N$ prompt tokens full: the two-token boundary accounts for much of the format effect; a 32-token tail preserves the question as well. (b) The same number of full tokens placed last, first, or at random on this template. (c) Reducing only the named tokens to two experts: the damage of the last eight is the damage of the last two, which are redundant with each other.\nL151: ### 3.3 Other families: one end or the other\nL152: Table cite99†1 summarises the same interventions at half of each model’s experts (details in Appendix Table cite103†14 and Figure cite104†5 ). In DeepSeek-V2-Lite the form loss is made at the other end: reducing only the first 20 tokens reproduces it ($-$9.45, family batch) while the sequence-start token alone is harmless (+0.11 [$-$0.54, +0.76]); what is left is the instruction, “Answer the question using the documents.\nL153: Give only the short answer.” Its tail acts in the opposite direction to Qwen3’s: reducing only the last two tokens shortens answers and raises F1 by 3.66. A vLLM prefix sweep on 400 questions (uniform loss $6.79$ in that batch) keeping the first 1, 8 or 32 tokens full recovers $-0.42$, $+3.80$ [$+1.88$, $+5.88$] and $+8.33$ [$+5.63$, $+11.21$] points against uniform reduction; a later run on the same manifest adds $-0.07$ for 2 tokens and $+1.39$ [$-0.36$, $+3.13$] for 4 (Appendix cite34†B ).\nL154: With ten to 300 words of unrelated text before the instruction, or with it between the documents, uniform reduction shows no detectable loss ($-1.9$ to $+1.8$; the ten-word interval [$-4.88$, $+2.01$] is wide; vLLM, 400 questions of the placement batch), so in DeepSeek’s standard layout we cannot tell whether the sensitivity follows the instruction or the prompt start.\nL155: Qwen1.5-MoE loses content rather than form—its answers get shorter and F1 rises, but the gold answer is found less often—and reducing only its last two tokens reproduces its containment loss, while keeping them full leaves about a quarter of it (Appendix Table cite103†14 ). OLMoE’s loss is spread over both ends. With 32 tokens kept full at each end, none of the four models shows a detectable HotpotQA loss in that batch (Qwen1.5’s F1 rises by 1.9 and its containment is unchanged; not an equivalence test).\nL156: Table 1: Where the loss is made, by family (HotpotQA, half the experts, earlier unmarked batch; change against the unmodified model with 95% intervals; DeepSeek’s 8–32 comes from the vLLM 0.31 prefix sweep of Section cite15†3.3 ). Qwen1.5’s loss is measured by containment (whether the gold answer appears), since its F1 rises.\nL157: Model  | Uniform reduction  | What is lost  | Where it is made  | First and last 32 full\nL158: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL159: Qwen3-30B-A3B  | F1 $-$3.34 [$-$4.45, $-$2.26]  | form  | last two tokens  | $-$0.41 [$-$1.23, +0.39]\nL160: DeepSeek-V2-Lite  | F1 $-$8.87 [$-$10.38, $-$7.37]  | form  | first 8–32 tokens (instruction or prompt start)  | +0.95 [$-$0.17, +2.12]\nL161: Qwen1.5-MoE-A2.7B  | containment $-$4.50 (F1 +2.13)  | content  | last two tokens  | containment +0.05 [$-$1.25, +1.35]\nL162: OLMoE-1B-7B  | F1 $-$5.11 [$-$6.76, $-$3.43]  | form (precision)  | both ends, beyond the last two  | $-$0.94 [$-$2.25, +0.34]\nL165: Three measurements on the unmodified model fit one account (Figure cite105†3 ). Attention. The query that predicts the first answer token attends to the second-to-last prompt token 15 times more per token than to a question token; attention is not importance, though, since the last token is read three times more than the second-to-last and matters far less when reduced (Section cite14†3.2 ). Path blocking.\nL180:  | DeepSeek-V2-Lite  | Qwen3-30B-A3B\nL181: --- | --- | ---\nL182: Is the sink disturbed? (change under uniform reduction)\nL183: ---\nL184: First token: attention / norm / largest channel  | $+0.3$% / $-0.2$% / $-0.1$%  | $+1.5$% / $-3.2$% / $-3.2$%\nL185: Last eight tokens: attention / norm / largest channel  | $-4.3$% / $-4.3$% / $-1.4$%  | $-0.3$% / $-8.3$% / $-10.0$%\nL186: Hide first token from last eight and answer, $\\Delta$NLL (random token)  | $+1.07$ ($+0.00$) nats  | $+40.6$ ($+0.01$) nats\nL539: Method  | HP sys  | HP std  | HP q-first  | 2Wiki  | LB HotpotQA  | MuSiQue  | MFQA  | Narr.  | Qasper  | LCC  | RepoB.\nL540: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL541: Uniform  | $-2.8$  | $-1.7$  | $-3.4$  | $-1.3$  | $+2.8$  | $-4.6$  | $-2.9$  | $-3.8$  | $-2.6$  | $-5.8$  | $-1.4$\nL542: Equal budget, ends  